$$
V_A = \frac{90 \text{km}}{\text{hr}} = 90 \times \frac{5}{18} \text{ m/s} = 25 \text{ ms}^{-1}
$$

$$
V_B = \frac{54 \text{km}}{\text{hr}} = 54 \times \frac{5}{18} \text{ m/s} = 15 \text{ ms}^{-1}
$$

Since the trains are moving in opposite directions, their relative velocity is the sum of their speeds.

$$
\vec{V}_{BA} = \vec{V}_B - \vec{V}_A = (15 - (-25)) \text{ ms}^{-1} = 40 \text{ ms}^{-1}
$$

$$
\text{Time of crossing} = \frac{\text{Length of train}}{\text{Relative velocity}}
$$

Given time of crossing is 8 s. Let the length of train B be \(\ell\).

$$
8 = \frac{\ell}{40}
$$

$$
\ell = 8 \times 40 = 320 \text{ meter}
$$
